<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gapsin Coup</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gapsin Coup was a failed attempt by Korean reformers led by Kim Ok-gyun to seize power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> in December 1884. With the help of Japanese legation guards, they killed conservative ministers at a banquet and announced a programme of modernising reforms. Chinese troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> crushed the coup within three days, and its leaders fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Japan then agreed in the Convention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a> to withdraw their troops.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imo-incident">Imo Incident</a> of 1882 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> intervened directly in Korean affairs. Trade regulations of October 1882 gave Chinese merchants advantages and extraterritorial rights, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> trained a new Capital Guards Command, and King Gojong could not appoint diplomats without Chinese approval. Queen Min's relatives, the Min clan, held key offices and pursued a pro-Chinese policy alongside the conservative Sadaedang, who favoured gradual change on the Chinese model. Against them stood the Enlightenment Party, young and well-educated men mostly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangban">yangban</a> class, among them Kim Okkyun, Pak Yung-hio, Hong Yeong-sik, Seo Gwang-beom and Philip Jaisohn, who admired Meiji Japan and wanted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> free of Chinese interference.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Shut out of the offices they needed, the reformers saw their chance when war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> over Annam led to the withdrawal of half the Chinese troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. On 4 December 1884, with the Japanese minister Takezoe Shinichiro promising the help of the legation guards, they struck during a banquet given by Hong Yeong-sik to celebrate the opening of the new national post office. Telling the king falsely that Chinese troops had caused a disturbance, they took him to a small palace and placed him under Japanese guard and killed or wounded several senior Sadaedang officials.</p><p>The new government drew up a 14-point programme: an end to tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, equal rights for all, a government close to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, land tax reform and a modern police. It had no more than 140 Japanese troops against at least 1,500 Chinese in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, and at Queen Min's secret request the Chinese attacked. Hong Yeong-sik was killed, the Japanese legation was burned and forty Japanese soldiers died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Gojong voided the reforms and demanded the conspirators back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Japan instead sent two battalions and seven warships, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanseong</a> of 9 January 1885 restored relations, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> paying ¥100,000 for the legation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Ito Hirobumi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> then concluded the convention of 31 May 1885, each side pledging to give notice before sending troops again. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s informal domination of Korea lasted until 1894, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> remaining in Seoul as Chinese Resident.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kapsin Coup” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Joseon became a battleground for Chinese, Japanese and Russian influence: the Imo Incident of 1882 and the <b>Gapsin Coup</b> of 1884 brought Chinese and Japanese troops into Seoul.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
